---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="About — Dylan McCurry">
  <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
  <h1>The job didn't disappear. It changed shape.</h1>

  <p>
    I spent fifteen years as a software engineer — five years in healthcare software at HCA, eight
    years as a consulting CMS engineer at Brainjocks, and the last five building for Xbox Player
    Experiences (XPE) on the Xbox Media team. Three very different domains, one constant: I wrote
    the code, reviewed the code, and shipped the code.
  </p>

  <p>
    Then Claude started writing a lot of the code. Faster than me, tirelessly, and — more often
    than I'd like to admit — pretty well. The honest reaction wasn't panic, it was recognition:
    this is a very capable, very junior engineer who needs direction, review, and guardrails. So I
    stopped competing with it and started managing it.
  </p>

  <h2>What "managing an AI" actually looks like</h2>
  <p>
    Concretely: I built a sidebar to see what it's doing (<a href="/projects/cmux-maestro">CMUX
    Maestro</a>), an app so nothing publishes without me looking at it first (<a href="/projects/pr-sniper">PR
    Sniper</a>), and a documented environment so the setup isn't just in my head (<a href="/projects/dotfiles">dotfiles</a>).
    Same job as managing a person: explain the task, check the work, decide what ships.
  </p>

  <p class="muted">
    MTSU grad (Middle Tennessee State University). Based around video games, bourbon, and metal —
    some things don't change.
  </p>

  <div style="margin-top: 2rem;">
    <a class="btn" href="/projects">See the tooling</a>
    <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/resume">Read the resume</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
